    "title": "How to watch San Francisco Giants vs. New York Mets",
    "text": "Giants' bats go silent behind Robbie Ray in series-opening loss to Reds originally appeared on NBC Sports Bay AreaWilly Adames was the Giants’ lone offense in a 2-1 loss to the Cincinnati Reds on Tuesday at Great American Ball Park.AdvertisementLeft-hander Robbie Ray made his fourth start of the 2026 MLB season and received little run support across his five-inning outing, striking out six, walking four and allowing two earned runs off home runs by Spencer Steer and Sal Stewart in the third and fourth innings, respectively.The Giants didn’t get on board against Cincinnati starter Brady Singer until the fifth, when their sole run in the series opener came on Adames’ solo shot to cut the Reds’ 2-0 lead in half. Singer finished the game with six innings pitched, six strikeouts, one strikeout, no walks and the one earned run.Right-handed reliever Caleb Kilian took over for Ray in the top of the sixth and overcame a leadoff walk to Matt McLain followed by an Elly De La Cruz single, striking out Stewart before forcing an inning-ending double play.AdvertisementAfter Keaton Winn retired the Reds in order to conclude the seventh inning, San Francisco got the tying run aboard in the top of the eighth when Luis Arraez singled on a line drive to center field. But the momentum faltered after Matt Chapman grounded into a double play.Ryan Walker kept the Reds’ bats silent in the bottom of the inning, giving the Giants a chance in their last at-bats of the game. But Jung Hoo Lee flew out to right field and Heliot Ramos struck out swinging, before Daniel Susac pinch-hit for Patrick Bailey and hit a deep fly ball to the warning track in left-center field for the game’s final out.Adames finished the game 2-for-4 with the home run and now is hitting .350 with seven hits, two homers, three RBI and four runs scored in his last 5 games. Arraez kept up his hot start to the season with a 3-for-4 day at the plate, and Lee was 2-for-4 with a two-out double in the fourth inning.With the loss, San Francisco fell to 6-11 on the season, while Cincinnati improved to 10-7.AdvertisementDownload and follow the Giants Talk Podcast",
